{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/0","value":{"id":"s1","num":1,"title":"Freeze the tree and confirm you are on the right commit","summary":"Establish a known-good, reproducible starting point before anything is built.","phase":"Pre-flight","duration":"2 min","owner":"release captain","done":false,"why":"A deploy is only meaningful if you can name the exact commit that is running in production. Starting from a dirty working tree means the artifact contains code that exists on nobody else's machine and in no git history. Confirming you are up to date with origin also catches the classic case where a teammate merged a fix ten minutes ago that you are about to silently overwrite or ship without.","skip":"You ship a binary nobody can reproduce. When an incident happens at 2am, `git checkout <sha>` gives you different code than what is live, so bisecting is impossible and the only fix is a blind forward-fix. You may also clobber a colleague's merge, causing a regression that already had a fix.","commands":"git status --porcelain            # must print nothing\ngit fetch --all --prune\ngit checkout main\ngit pull --ff-only origin main\ngit rev-parse --short HEAD         # note this SHA, you will need it","verify":"git status --porcelain | wc -l    # expect 0\ngit log --oneline -1              # expect the same SHA as origin/main","gate":"Gate: working tree clean + local HEAD == origin/main"}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/1","value":{"id":"s2","num":2,"title":"Install dependencies from the lockfile only","summary":"Reproduce the exact dependency graph CI validated — no floating versions.","phase":"Pre-flight","duration":"3 min","owner":"release captain","done":false,"why":"`npm install` is allowed to resolve new semver-compatible versions and rewrite the lockfile. `npm ci` deletes node_modules and installs the exact tree recorded in package-lock.json, which is the same tree your tests passed against. Reproducible installs are the difference between 'the build is deterministic' and 'the build depends on what day it is'.","skip":"A transitive dependency publishes a patch release with a breaking change, your local build silently picks it up, and production gets code that was never tested. You also get 'works on my machine' bugs that vanish when anyone else pulls, because their node_modules differs from yours.","commands":"node --version                    # must match .nvmrc / engines field\nrm -rf node_modules\nnpm ci                            # lockfile-exact install, fails if lock is stale\nnpm audit --audit-level=high      # block on high/critical CVEs","verify":"git diff --exit-code package-lock.json   # lockfile must be unchanged\nnpm ls --depth=0                          # no UNMET DEPENDENCY lines","gate":"Gate: lockfile untouched, no high-severity advisories"}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/2","value":{"id":"s3","num":3,"title":"Run the full test, lint and type-check suite locally","summary":"Catch failures on your laptop where they cost seconds, not in production where they cost users.","phase":"Validation","duration":"6 min","owner":"author","done":false,"why":"Tests, linting and static types each catch a different class of defect: tests catch wrong behaviour, types catch wrong shapes at boundaries, lint catches unsafe patterns. Running them before you push shortens the feedback loop from minutes (CI queue) to seconds, and running them locally with the same commands CI uses means a green local run genuinely predicts a green pipeline.","skip":"You burn a CI slot to learn about a typo, or worse, a flaky suite gets marked 'probably fine' and a real regression rides along. Type errors that only appear at runtime become 500s on the request path — undefined property reads in a payment handler are exactly the bug this step exists to stop.","commands":"npm run lint\nnpm run typecheck\nnpm test -- --runInBand --coverage\nnpm run test:e2e -- --headless      # optional but recommended pre-release","verify":"echo $?                             # expect 0 after each command\n# coverage must not drop below the threshold in jest.config.js","gate":"Gate: all suites green, coverage >= 80%"}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/3","value":{"id":"s4","num":4,"title":"Build the production artifact and inspect it","summary":"Compile once, then promote the same artifact through every environment.","phase":"Build","duration":"4 min","owner":"CI","done":false,"why":"Production builds differ from dev builds: minification, tree-shaking, NODE_ENV=production, dead-code elimination and different environment variables. Bugs that only exist in the optimised bundle are common. Building the artifact once and promoting that exact image to staging then production removes an entire category of 'staging passed but production failed' incidents, because the bytes are identical.","skip":"You discover a minifier bug, a missing env var, or a bundle that ballooned to 8 MB only after real users hit it. If you rebuild separately for each environment, staging and production run different code and staging stops being a meaningful test.","commands":"export NODE_ENV=production\nnpm run build\ndu -sh dist/                        # sanity-check bundle size\ndocker build -t registry.example.com/app:$(git rev-parse --short HEAD) .\ndocker push registry.example.com/app:$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)","verify":"ls -la dist/\ndocker run --rm registry.example.com/app:$(git rev-parse --short HEAD) node -e \"console.log('boot ok')\"","gate":"Gate: image pushed and tagged with the commit SHA"}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/4","value":{"id":"s5","num":5,"title":"Apply database migrations backward-compatibly","summary":"Migrate the schema first, in a way that both the old and new code can run against.","phase":"Data","duration":"5 min","owner":"DBA + release captain","done":false,"why":"During any rolling deploy, old and new application code run at the same time. That means every migration must be additive and backward-compatible: add nullable columns, backfill, then switch reads, and only drop the old column in a later release. Taking a backup first gives you a recovery path, and running migrations as a separate step from the code deploy means you can roll back the code without also rolling back data.","skip":"Renaming or dropping a column in the same release that changes the code guarantees errors for every request served by the not-yet-updated pods. Without a pre-migration backup, a bad `UPDATE` with a missing WHERE clause is unrecoverable — that is a data-loss incident, not a deploy hiccup.","commands":"pg_dump \"$DATABASE_URL\" --format=custom --file=backup-$(date +%F-%H%M).dump\nnpm run migrate:status              # list pending migrations\nnpm run migrate:up -- --dry-run     # print the SQL, read every line\nnpm run migrate:up","verify":"npm run migrate:status              # expect 'no pending migrations'\npsql \"$DATABASE_URL\" -c \"\\d+ users\"  # confirm new columns are nullable","gate":"Gate: backup stored off-host, migrations additive only"}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/5","value":{"id":"s6","num":6,"title":"Deploy to staging and run smoke tests","summary":"Prove the artifact boots and serves real traffic against real infrastructure.","phase":"Staging","duration":"5 min","owner":"release captain","done":false,"why":"Unit tests mock the world; staging does not. This is the first time your artifact meets real DNS, TLS termination, secret injection, connection pools, third-party APIs and the migrated schema. Smoke tests should cover the two or three flows whose failure would force an immediate rollback — login, checkout, and a health endpoint — not the whole regression suite.","skip":"A missing production secret, an unreachable Redis host, or a broken health check surfaces directly in production. These failures are invisible to unit tests because every one of them lives in configuration, which tests mock away by design.","commands":"kubectl config use-context staging\nkubectl set image deploy/app app=registry.example.com/app:$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\nkubectl rollout status deploy/app --timeout=120s\nnpm run smoke -- --base-url https://staging.example.com","verify":"curl -fsS https://staging.example.com/healthz\nkubectl logs deploy/app --tail=50 | grep -i error   # expect no output","gate":"Gate: healthz 200, smoke suite green, no error logs"}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/6","value":{"id":"s7","num":7,"title":"Release to production behind a canary","summary":"Expose the new version to a small slice of traffic before everyone gets it.","phase":"Release","duration":"6 min","owner":"release captain","done":false,"why":"Staging traffic is synthetic and low-volume. A canary sends 5–10% of real production traffic to the new version so that load-dependent problems — connection pool exhaustion, N+1 queries, cache stampedes, memory leaks — reveal themselves while 90% of users are still on the known-good version. Blast radius control is the entire point: you are buying the option to abort cheaply.","skip":"A 100% cutover means a latency regression or crash loop hits every user at once. Mean time to detection stays the same, but the number of affected customers is ten to twenty times higher, and you will be debugging under maximum pressure with support tickets piling up.","commands":"kubectl config use-context production\nkubectl apply -f k8s/canary.yaml    # weight: 10\nkubectl set image deploy/app-canary app=registry.example.com/app:$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\nkubectl rollout status deploy/app-canary --timeout=120s\n# after 10 clean minutes, promote:\nkubectl set image deploy/app app=registry.example.com/app:$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\nkubectl rollout status deploy/app --timeout=300s","verify":"kubectl get pods -l app=app -o wide        # all Running, 0 restarts\ncurl -fsS https://example.com/healthz | jq .version   # expect the new SHA","gate":"Gate: canary error rate <= baseline for 10 minutes"}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/7","value":{"id":"s8","num":8,"title":"Monitor, tag the release, and write the rollback note","summary":"Watch the golden signals, then make this release findable and reversible forever.","phase":"Post-deploy","duration":"4 min + 30 min watch","owner":"release captain","done":false,"why":"Most deploy-induced incidents show up within the first thirty minutes, so an active watch on error rate, p95 latency, saturation and traffic is the cheapest incident detection you will ever get. Tagging the commit and recording the previous image gives anyone — including future you at 2am — a one-command path back to the last known-good state without needing to reconstruct history.","skip":"A slow memory leak or a 2% error-rate bump goes unnoticed until customers report it hours later. Without a tag and a recorded previous image, rolling back becomes archaeology: guessing which SHA was live, and every minute of that guessing is downtime.","commands":"git tag -a v1.8.0 -m \"Release v1.8.0 ($(git rev-parse --short HEAD))\"\ngit push origin v1.8.0\nkubectl rollout history deploy/app          # record the previous revision number\n# keep this handy for 30 minutes:\nkubectl rollout undo deploy/app --to-revision=<previous>","verify":"watch -n 30 'curl -fsS https://example.com/healthz'\n# dashboards: error rate < 0.5%, p95 < 400ms, CPU < 70% for 30 min","gate":"Gate: 30 minutes clean, tag pushed, rollback command pasted in the channel"}
{"op":"add","path":"/elements/rollback-card","value":{"type":"Card","props":{"title":"Emergency rollback","description":"If any gate fails, stop the promotion and run these immediately — do not debug forward in production."},"children":["rollback-stack"]}}
