{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps","value":[]}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/0","value":{"id":"s1","num":1,"title":"Freeze the branch and cut an immutable tag","summary":"Pin exactly what you are shipping before anything else moves.","why":"A deploy must reference an immutable artifact, not a moving branch. If you build from 'main' while teammates keep merging, the code you tested is not the code that ships, and the commit you would roll back to no longer exists in a clean state. A signed annotated tag gives you one stable coordinate that logs, images, and incident reviews can all point at.","skip":"You lose traceability. Two engineers deploying 'main' ten minutes apart ship different code under the same release name, and during an incident you cannot answer the single most important question: what exactly is running in production right now?","commands":"git checkout main\ngit pull --ff-only origin main\ngit log --oneline -5\ngit tag -a v2.14.0 -m \"Release v2.14.0\"\ngit push origin v2.14.0","verify":"`git describe --tags` prints v2.14.0 and the tag is visible on the remote.","done":false}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/1","value":{"id":"s2","num":2,"title":"Install locked dependencies and run the full test suite","summary":"Reproduce the build environment and prove the tag is green.","why":"Installing from a lockfile (`npm ci`, not `npm install`) guarantees byte-identical dependency trees between CI, your machine, and the production image. Running lint, type-check, and tests against the tag — not against your dirty working tree — is the last cheap gate before defects become customer-visible.","skip":"A transitive dependency silently upgrades and introduces a breaking change that only appears in production. Or a test that was red on the tag ships anyway, and you spend the outage debugging a bug CI already knew about.","commands":"git checkout v2.14.0\nnpm ci\nnpm run lint\nnpm run typecheck\nnpm test -- --run --coverage","verify":"All suites pass with exit code 0 and coverage is at or above the agreed threshold.","done":false}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/2","value":{"id":"s3","num":3,"title":"Build and push the production image","summary":"Create one artifact, tagged by commit SHA, that every environment will reuse.","why":"Build once, promote many. If staging and production build separately, they can differ in base image, native modules, or build-time env vars — so a staging pass proves nothing. Tagging by immutable commit SHA (in addition to a human tag) means you can always trace a running container back to source.","skip":"You get 'works in staging, breaks in prod' bugs caused purely by build drift, and rolling back becomes a rebuild (slow, and it may not even reproduce) instead of repointing to a previous image digest.","commands":"export SHA=$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\ndocker build --platform linux/amd64 -t registry.example.com/app:$SHA -t registry.example.com/app:v2.14.0 .\ndocker push registry.example.com/app:$SHA\ndocker push registry.example.com/app:v2.14.0\ndocker inspect --format='{{index .RepoDigests 0}}' registry.example.com/app:$SHA","verify":"The registry lists both tags and you have recorded the image digest (sha256:…) in the release ticket.","done":false}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/3","value":{"id":"s4","num":4,"title":"Back up the database, then run migrations","summary":"Take a restorable snapshot first; apply only backward-compatible migrations.","why":"Code can be rolled back in seconds; data cannot. A verified backup taken immediately before migration is your only true undo. Migrations must also be backward compatible (add columns nullable, backfill separately, drop columns in a later release) because during a rolling deploy the old and new code run at the same time against the same schema.","skip":"A destructive migration plus a failed deploy equals permanent data loss, or the old pods start throwing 500s the moment a column they still SELECT disappears. Restoring from last night's backup means losing a day of customer writes.","commands":"pg_dump \"$DATABASE_URL\" --format=custom --file=backup-v2.14.0-$(date +%F-%H%M).dump\nls -lh backup-v2.14.0-*.dump\nnpm run migrate:status\nnpm run migrate:up\nnpm run migrate:status","verify":"The dump file exists and is non-trivial in size, and migrate:status shows zero pending migrations.","done":false}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/4","value":{"id":"s5","num":5,"title":"Sync configuration and secrets","summary":"Make sure every variable the new code reads actually exists in the target environment.","why":"New releases frequently introduce new env vars. Config lives outside the image on purpose, so it will not arrive with the deploy — you must push it. Diffing the required keys against what is set catches the single most common deploy failure: a container that crash-loops on startup because one secret is missing.","skip":"Pods enter CrashLoopBackOff on boot, or worse, the app starts with a silently defaulted value — pointing at the wrong queue, the wrong payment key, or an empty feature flag — and corrupts data while looking healthy.","commands":"kubectl -n prod get secret app-env -o jsonpath='{.data}' | jq 'keys'\nkubectl -n prod create secret generic app-env \\\n  --from-env-file=.env.production \\\n  --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -\ndiff <(grep -o '^[A-Z_]*' .env.production.example | sort) \\\n     <(grep -o '^[A-Z_]*' .env.production | sort)","verify":"The diff is empty and the secret contains every key the release notes list as new.","done":false}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/5","value":{"id":"s6","num":6,"title":"Deploy to staging and run smoke tests","summary":"Exercise the exact image against production-shaped data before real users see it.","why":"Staging is the only place where the real artifact, real migrations, and real config meet without customer risk. Automated smoke tests on the critical paths — login, checkout, one write, one read — catch integration failures that unit tests structurally cannot (bad DNS, expired cert, wrong bucket, broken third-party contract).","skip":"Production becomes your test environment. You discover the misconfigured CDN or the broken OAuth callback from customer support tickets instead of from a test run that costs three minutes.","commands":"kubectl -n staging set image deploy/app app=registry.example.com/app:$SHA\nkubectl -n staging rollout status deploy/app --timeout=180s\ncurl -fsS https://staging.example.com/healthz\nnpm run test:smoke -- --base-url=https://staging.example.com","verify":"Rollout completes, /healthz returns 200, and every smoke test passes.","done":false}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/6","value":{"id":"s7","num":7,"title":"Promote to production with a rolling deploy","summary":"Shift traffic gradually with readiness probes and a surge/unavailable budget.","why":"A rolling update replaces pods a few at a time and only sends traffic to instances that pass their readiness probe, so capacity never dips and a bad build is caught before it owns 100% of requests. Watching the rollout synchronously means you are present at the exact moment things can go wrong — that is when a rollback is cheap.","skip":"Replacing all pods at once causes a hard outage window and, if the new image is broken, a total outage rather than a degraded slice. Deploying and walking away means the rollback happens minutes or hours later, after the error budget is already spent.","commands":"kubectl -n prod set image deploy/app app=registry.example.com/app:$SHA --record\nkubectl -n prod rollout status deploy/app --timeout=300s\nkubectl -n prod get pods -l app=app -o wide\nkubectl -n prod logs -l app=app --since=2m --tail=50","verify":"All replicas are Ready on the new image and logs show no startup errors or elevated exceptions.","done":false}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/7","value":{"id":"s8","num":8,"title":"Verify, monitor, and close the release","summary":"Watch the golden signals for 15–30 minutes, then announce and document.","why":"Most regressions surface under real traffic, not at boot: latency creep from an un-indexed query, error spikes on a rare code path, memory growth that only paginates after a few minutes. A deliberate observation window turns a potential incident into a quiet rollback. Recording the tag, digest, and migration state makes the next person's job — possibly yours at 3am — tractable.","skip":"You declare success on a green rollout and go home; the p99 latency doubled and the on-call engineer inherits an unexplained page with no link between the symptom and your release.","commands":"curl -fsS https://app.example.com/healthz\ncurl -o /dev/null -s -w \"%{http_code} %{time_total}s\\n\" https://app.example.com/\nkubectl -n prod top pods -l app=app\n# watch dashboards: error rate, p95/p99 latency, saturation, queue depth\ngh release create v2.14.0 --generate-notes","verify":"Error rate and latency match the pre-deploy baseline for 15+ minutes and the release notes are published.","done":false}
{"op":"add","path":"/elements/rollback","value":{"type":"Card","props":{"title":"Rollback procedure","description":"Decide fast: if the golden signals are degraded and the cause is not obvious within 5 minutes, roll back first and investigate after."},"children":["rollback-alert","rollback-cmd","rollback-note"]}}
{"op":"add","path":"/elements/rollback-alert","value":{"type":"Alert","props":{"title":"Rolling back code does not roll back data","message":"If step 4 applied a destructive migration, a code rollback alone will break the old release. Restore from the dump you took, or ship a forward-fix instead.","type":"error"},"children":[]}}
{"op":"add","path":"/elements/rollback-cmd","value":{"type":"Text","props":{"variant":"code","text":"kubectl -n prod rollout undo deploy/app\nkubectl -n prod rollout status deploy/app --timeout=300s\nkubectl -n prod get deploy/app -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}'\n\n# if the schema must also revert:\nnpm run migrate:down -- --step=1\n# or full restore from the pre-deploy snapshot:\npg_restore --clean --if-exists --dbname=\"$DATABASE_URL\" backup-v2.14.0-*.dump"},"children":[]}}
{"op":"add","path":"/elements/rollback-note","value":{"type":"Text","props":{"text":"After any rollback, open an incident note with the tag, image digest, symptom, and the time traffic recovered.","variant":"muted"},"children":[]}}
{"op":"add","path":"/elements/faq","value":{"type":"Accordion","props":{"type":"multiple","items":[{"title":"Rollout is stuck in Progressing — what now?","content":"Run `kubectl -n prod describe pod -l app=app` and read the Events. The usual causes are ImagePullBackOff (wrong tag or registry credentials), a failing readiness probe (app listening on the wrong port or slow boot — raise initialDelaySeconds), or Insufficient cpu/memory (no room to schedule the surge pod). Fix the cause or run `kubectl rollout undo`."},{"title":"Why `npm ci` instead of `npm install`?","content":"`npm install` may resolve newer versions and rewrite the lockfile, so your build is not reproducible. `npm ci` installs the exact lockfile tree and fails loudly if package.json and package-lock.json disagree — exactly the behaviour you want in a release pipeline."},{"title":"Do I really need staging if the tests pass?","content":"Yes. Tests validate logic; staging validates the environment — DNS, TLS, secrets, network policy, third-party sandboxes, migration timing against realistic data volume. Those failure classes are invisible to unit tests."},{"title":"How long should I watch after deploying?","content":"At least 15 minutes, and through one full cache/session expiry cycle if you changed serialization or auth. Latency and memory regressions are time-delayed; error-rate regressions usually appear within two minutes."},{"title":"Can I combine a schema change and the code that uses it?","content":"Only if the change is additive and safe for the old code running concurrently. Renames and drops need the expand/contract pattern: release N adds and dual-writes, release N+1 reads the new shape, release N+2 drops the old column."}]},"children":[]}}
