{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/0","value":{"id":"s1","num":1,"title":"Freeze the branch and tag the release","summary":"Pin exactly which commit is going out and give it an immutable name.","phase":"Prepare","time":"3 min","owner":"release manager","risk":"medium","why":"A deploy is only debuggable if you can say precisely which commit is running. Tagging creates an immutable pointer that survives force-pushes, rebases and branch deletions, and it is what your rollback command will target an hour from now. Freezing main also stops a teammate from merging an unrelated change into the middle of your release, which would silently widen the diff you are about to ship.","skip":"You deploy 'main' as a moving target. If an incident starts 20 minutes later you cannot tell whether the bad code is yours or the PR that landed while you were deploying, and `git revert` has nothing stable to point at. Rollback becomes archaeology instead of one command.","commands":"git checkout main\ngit pull --ff-only origin main\ngit log --oneline -5            # confirm the HEAD you expect\ngit tag -a v2.7.0 -m \"Release v2.7.0\"\ngit push origin v2.7.0\ngh api -X PUT repos/:owner/:repo/branches/main/protection/enforce_admins  # freeze merges","verify":"`git ls-remote --tags origin | grep v2.7.0` prints the tag, and the repo's branch bar shows main as protected/frozen."}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/1","value":{"id":"s2","num":2,"title":"Run the full test suite and linters in CI","summary":"Green pipeline on the exact tag, not on your laptop.","phase":"Verify","time":"8 min","owner":"any engineer","risk":"high","why":"CI runs on a clean machine with production-like dependency versions, so it catches the 'works on my machine' class of bugs: uncommitted files, stale local caches, missing migrations, native modules pinned to your OS. Running it against the tag (not the branch) proves the exact bytes you are shipping pass, and produces the test report you'll attach to the change record.","skip":"A broken build reaches users. The cheapest failure to catch — a typo, a failing unit test, a type error — becomes a production incident that costs 50x more to diagnose under pressure, usually with a partially-migrated database behind it.","commands":"git checkout v2.7.0\nnpm ci                          # clean install from lockfile, never npm install\nnpm run lint\nnpm run typecheck\nnpm test -- --run --coverage\ngh run watch                    # follow the CI run for the tag","verify":"Exit code 0 from every command, and the GitHub Actions run for tag v2.7.0 shows all jobs green."}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/2","value":{"id":"s3","num":3,"title":"Build the immutable, versioned artifact","summary":"One container image, built once, promoted through every environment.","phase":"Build","time":"6 min","owner":"CI","risk":"high","why":"Building once and promoting the same digest to staging and production removes environment drift entirely: the thing you tested is literally the thing that runs. Tagging the image with the git SHA makes 'what is running right now?' answerable, and pushing to the registry gives you an artifact you can roll back to instantly without a rebuild.","skip":"Each environment builds its own image, so staging and prod quietly differ by a transitive dependency that moved. Your 'tested' code was never the code that shipped, and a rollback requires a 6-minute rebuild you have to do while the site is down.","commands":"export SHA=$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\ndocker build --platform linux/amd64 \\\n  -t registry.example.com/api:v2.7.0 \\\n  -t registry.example.com/api:$SHA .\ndocker push registry.example.com/api:v2.7.0\ndocker push registry.example.com/api:$SHA\ndocker inspect --format='{{index .RepoDigests 0}}' registry.example.com/api:v2.7.0","verify":"The push prints a sha256 digest; record it. `docker run --rm registry.example.com/api:v2.7.0 --version` prints 2.7.0."}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/3","value":{"id":"s4","num":4,"title":"Take a verified database backup","summary":"A restorable snapshot taken minutes before any schema change.","phase":"Safety","time":"5 min","owner":"on-call DBA","risk":"critical","why":"Code rolls back in seconds; data does not. A migration that drops a column, a bad backfill, or a UPDATE without a WHERE clause is unrecoverable unless a fresh snapshot exists. Taking it immediately before the migration bounds your worst-case data loss to the length of the deploy, and actually listing/restore-testing the dump proves the backup is not a zero-byte file.","skip":"Your rollback plan is a hope. One destructive migration and you are restoring last night's nightly backup, losing a full day of customer writes — the kind of incident that ends up in a public postmortem and a support-credit bill.","commands":"export STAMP=$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ)\npg_dump \"$DATABASE_URL\" -Fc -f backup-$STAMP.dump\nls -lh backup-$STAMP.dump                      # must be non-trivial in size\npg_restore --list backup-$STAMP.dump | head    # proves the dump is readable\naws s3 cp backup-$STAMP.dump s3://prod-backups/pre-v2.7.0/","verify":"`pg_restore --list` prints table entries and the S3 object size matches the local file."}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/4","value":{"id":"s5","num":5,"title":"Apply schema migrations (backward-compatible only)","summary":"Migrate before the new code ships, with additive changes only.","phase":"Data","time":"4 min","owner":"release manager","risk":"critical","why":"During a rolling deploy old and new code run simultaneously, so the schema must satisfy both. Additive-only migrations (add nullable column, add index CONCURRENTLY, backfill in batches) let the old version keep working while the new version starts using the new field. Destructive changes — drops and renames — belong in a follow-up release after all old pods are gone. Reviewing the SQL with a dry run catches accidental table rewrites that would lock a hot table.","skip":"Either the new code boots and immediately 500s on a column that does not exist, or a blocking ALTER takes an exclusive lock on your busiest table and every request times out. Both look identical to users: total outage.","commands":"npm run migrate:status                 # list pending migrations\nnpm run migrate:dry-run | tee migration-plan.sql\ngrep -Ei 'drop |rename |not null' migration-plan.sql   # must be empty\nnpm run migrate:up\nnpm run migrate:status                 # all migrations applied","verify":"migrate:status shows zero pending, and `SELECT count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE wait_event_type='Lock';` returns 0."}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/5","value":{"id":"s6","num":6,"title":"Deploy to staging and run smoke tests","summary":"Full dress rehearsal of the same image against production-shaped config.","phase":"Rehearsal","time":"6 min","owner":"any engineer","risk":"high","why":"Unit tests cannot catch a missing environment variable, an unrotated secret, a wrong feature-flag default, a broken health-check path or a bad reverse-proxy rule. Staging exercises the real startup path, the real migrations and the real integrations, so config mistakes fail in front of nobody. Running the smoke suite against staging also validates your smoke suite itself before you rely on it in production.","skip":"Your users become the test environment. The most common deploy failure by far is configuration, not code, and it surfaces as a crash-loop 30 seconds after the production rollout starts — exactly when you have the least time to think.","commands":"kubectl -n staging set image deploy/api api=registry.example.com/api:v2.7.0\nkubectl -n staging rollout status deploy/api --timeout=180s\ncurl -fsS https://staging.example.com/healthz\nnpm run test:smoke -- --base-url=https://staging.example.com\nkubectl -n staging logs deploy/api --tail=100 | grep -i error","verify":"Rollout completes, /healthz returns 200 with the new version string, smoke suite passes, no ERROR lines in logs."}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/6","value":{"id":"s7","num":7,"title":"Deploy to production as a canary, then roll forward","summary":"Send 5% of traffic to the new version, watch, then complete the rollout.","phase":"Release","time":"8 min","owner":"release manager","risk":"critical","why":"A canary converts a potential 100% outage into a 5% blip. Real production traffic has request shapes, data volumes and cache-miss patterns staging never reproduces, so you want a small blast radius while those hit for the first time. Watching error rate and p99 latency for a few minutes at low traffic gives you a cheap, reversible decision point before the whole fleet is on new code. maxUnavailable=0 keeps capacity constant so a partial rollout never sheds load.","skip":"Every user meets the bug at the same instant. A 2% error-rate regression that a canary would have shown you as a handful of failures becomes thousands of failed checkouts, and you are now debugging a full outage instead of aborting a canary.","commands":"kubectl -n prod apply -f k8s/canary.yaml      # 1 replica, 5% weight\nkubectl -n prod rollout status deploy/api-canary --timeout=180s\nwatch -n 10 'curl -s https://api.example.com/metrics | grep -E \"http_5xx|latency_p99\"'\n# after 5 clean minutes:\nkubectl -n prod set image deploy/api api=registry.example.com/api:v2.7.0\nkubectl -n prod rollout status deploy/api --timeout=600s\nkubectl -n prod delete -f k8s/canary.yaml","verify":"Canary 5xx rate stays at baseline for 5 minutes, then `kubectl rollout status` reports all replicas updated and available."}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/7","value":{"id":"s8","num":8,"title":"Verify, monitor for 30 minutes, then announce","summary":"Prove the release is healthy, unfreeze the branch, and write it down.","phase":"Confirm","time":"10 min + 30 min watch","owner":"release manager","risk":"medium","why":"'Deployed' is not 'working'. Many regressions only appear after caches expire, a cron fires, or a queue backs up — typically 5–30 minutes in. An explicit watch period with named dashboards and thresholds means someone is accountable for noticing. Announcing the release with the tag and image digest gives support and the next on-call the context to correlate any later report, and unfreezing main lets the team resume work.","skip":"A silent regression lives in production until a customer reports it days later, by which time three more releases have shipped on top and nobody can bisect it. Support has no idea what changed, and the next engineer to deploy inherits an unexplained error-rate baseline.","commands":"curl -fsS https://api.example.com/version        # expect 2.7.0\nnpm run test:smoke -- --base-url=https://api.example.com\nkubectl -n prod get pods -l app=api              # all Running, 0 restarts\nopen \"https://grafana.example.com/d/api-slo?from=now-1h\"\ngh release create v2.7.0 --generate-notes\ngh api -X DELETE repos/:owner/:repo/branches/main/protection/enforce_admins  # unfreeze","verify":"Error rate, p99 latency and queue depth are at pre-deploy baseline after 30 minutes; release notes published and #deploys notified."}
