{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/0","value":{"id":"s1","number":1,"title":"Freeze the branch and tag the release","stage":"Prepare","owner":"Release manager","minutes":5,"risk":"unreproducible build","summary":"Cut an immutable git tag from a green main so every later artifact points at one known commit.","why":"A deploy is only debuggable if you can name exactly what shipped. Tagging creates an immutable pointer to one commit SHA, so the image, the migration set, the changelog and the rollback target all refer to the same code. The freeze prevents someone merging a new PR halfway through your rollout, which would otherwise mean the image you tested and the image you ship are different.","ifSkipped":"You deploy a moving target. When an error spikes at 14:20 you cannot answer 'what code is running?', bisecting is impossible, and rollback has no known-good tag to return to — you end up reverting commits live under pressure. Teams that skip tagging routinely ship an untested commit that landed on main minutes before the build.","commands":"git checkout main\ngit pull --ff-only origin main\ngit log --oneline -5              # confirm the expected HEAD\ngit tag -a v2.14.0 -m \"Release v2.14.0\"\ngit push origin v2.14.0\ngh pr list --state open --label \"blocks-release\"   # must be empty","verify":"`git describe --tags --exact-match` prints v2.14.0 and the tag appears in the remote: `git ls-remote --tags origin | grep v2.14.0`.","done":false}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/1","value":{"id":"s2","number":2,"title":"Run the full test suite and static checks in CI","stage":"Verify","owner":"CI","minutes":10,"risk":"known bugs shipped","summary":"Run unit, integration, lint, type and security checks against the tagged commit — not against your laptop.","why":"CI runs in a clean, reproducible environment with the same dependency versions as production. Local runs hide problems: stale caches, uncommitted files, different Node/Python versions, and environment variables that only exist on your machine. Running against the tag (not your branch) proves the exact artifact you are about to build is green.","ifSkipped":"You ship regressions that a 90-second test run would have caught, and you find them via customer error reports instead. Worse, dependency and lint gates also cover security scanning — skipping CI is how a vulnerable transitive dependency reaches production unnoticed.","commands":"git checkout v2.14.0\nnpm ci\nnpm run lint\nnpm run typecheck\nnpm test -- --runInBand --coverage\nnpm audit --audit-level=high\n# or trigger it remotely and watch:\ngh workflow run ci.yml --ref v2.14.0\ngh run watch","verify":"All jobs green in `gh run list --limit 1`; coverage did not drop below the 80% gate; `npm audit` reports 0 high/critical findings.","done":false}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/2","value":{"id":"s3","number":3,"title":"Build the container image and push it to the registry","stage":"Build","owner":"CI","minutes":6,"risk":"drift between test and prod","summary":"Build once, tag with the release version and the commit SHA, then push to the registry.","why":"Building a single immutable image and promoting it through environments guarantees that staging and production run byte-identical bits. Tagging with both the version and the immutable SHA gives you a human-readable name and a forgery-proof one. Pushing to a registry (rather than building on the host) means the cluster can pull the same digest on every node and during every restart.","ifSkipped":"If you build per-environment you get drift: staging passes, production fails on a different base-image patch level. If you deploy from a local build or a mutable `latest` tag, autoscaling later pulls a *different* image than the one you validated, producing the classic 'it worked when we deployed it' mystery hours after the fact.","commands":"export SHA=$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\ndocker build --pull -t ghcr.io/acme/api-gateway:v2.14.0 -t ghcr.io/acme/api-gateway:$SHA .\ndocker login ghcr.io -u \"$GH_USER\" --password-stdin < ~/.ghcr-token\ndocker push ghcr.io/acme/api-gateway:v2.14.0\ndocker push ghcr.io/acme/api-gateway:$SHA\ncosign sign ghcr.io/acme/api-gateway:v2.14.0","verify":"`docker manifest inspect ghcr.io/acme/api-gateway:v2.14.0` returns a digest; record that digest — it is what you will roll back to or from.","done":false}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/3","value":{"id":"s4","number":4,"title":"Take a verified backup of the database","stage":"Safety","owner":"DBA","minutes":4,"risk":"irreversible data loss","summary":"Snapshot the database immediately before migrations and confirm the snapshot is restorable.","why":"Code rollbacks are cheap; data rollbacks are not. A migration that drops a column, rewrites rows, or fails halfway can corrupt state that no image rollback will fix. Taking the snapshot immediately before the migration keeps the recovery point tight, and verifying it matters because an unrestorable backup is just a comforting file.","ifSkipped":"A bad migration becomes a permanent incident: you cannot revert schema changes that destroyed data, and your recovery window jumps from minutes to 'whatever last night's nightly backup contains' — meaning you knowingly discard a day of customer writes. This is the step whose absence turns a 10-minute rollback into a multi-day data-recovery project.","commands":"aws rds create-db-snapshot \\\n  --db-instance-identifier prod-api-db \\\n  --db-snapshot-identifier pre-v2-14-0-$(date +%Y%m%d%H%M)\naws rds wait db-snapshot-available \\\n  --db-snapshot-identifier pre-v2-14-0-$(date +%Y%m%d%H%M)\n# logical backup as a second copy\npg_dump \"$DATABASE_URL\" -Fc -f pre-v2.14.0.dump\npg_restore --list pre-v2.14.0.dump | head    # proves the dump is readable","verify":"Snapshot status is `available`, `pg_restore --list` prints a table of contents, and the dump size is within ~5% of yesterday's backup.","done":false}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/4","value":{"id":"s5","number":5,"title":"Run database migrations (backward compatible only)","stage":"Migrate","owner":"Backend","minutes":3,"risk":"downtime during rollout","summary":"Apply additive migrations before the new code ships, inside a transaction, with a lock timeout.","why":"During a rolling deploy old and new pods run at the same time, so the schema must satisfy both. Additive-only migrations (add nullable column, add index concurrently, backfill later, drop in a *later* release) let both versions read and write safely. A short `lock_timeout` prevents a migration from queueing behind a long query and silently blocking every write on the table.","ifSkipped":"Skip the migration and the new pods crash-loop on missing columns. Run a destructive one instead and the still-running old pods 500 on every request to the dropped field. Run it without a lock timeout and a single ALTER TABLE can freeze writes cluster-wide — a self-inflicted outage that looks exactly like a database failure.","commands":"psql \"$DATABASE_URL\" -c \"SET lock_timeout='5s'; SET statement_timeout='60s';\"\nnpm run migrate:status            # review pending migrations\nnpm run migrate:up -- --dry-run   # print the SQL, read every line\nnpm run migrate:up\npsql \"$DATABASE_URL\" -c \"\\d+ users\"   # confirm new columns are nullable","verify":"`npm run migrate:status` shows zero pending migrations, no long-lived locks in `pg_locks`, and error rate on the old pods is unchanged.","done":false}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/5","value":{"id":"s6","number":6,"title":"Deploy to staging and run the smoke suite","stage":"Rehearse","owner":"Deployer","minutes":6,"risk":"config and secret failures","summary":"Promote the exact image to staging and exercise the critical user journeys against it.","why":"Staging is where you test the *deployment*, not the code — manifests, env vars, secrets, migrations against real-shaped data, third-party credentials, and readiness probes. It is a rehearsal of the same commands you are about to run in production, so any typo in a manifest or missing secret surfaces where nobody is paying you money.","ifSkipped":"Configuration bugs get discovered by customers. The most common ones are invisible in unit tests: a missing secret so the pod boots and then fails on first request, a wrong queue URL so jobs vanish, or a readiness probe path that never returns 200 so the rollout stalls at 50% with traffic split across broken pods.","commands":"kubectl config use-context staging-us-east-1\nkubectl set image deploy/api-gateway api=ghcr.io/acme/api-gateway:v2.14.0 -n staging\nkubectl rollout status deploy/api-gateway -n staging --timeout=180s\nnpm run test:smoke -- --base-url https://staging.api.acme.com\ncurl -fsS https://staging.api.acme.com/healthz | jq .","verify":"Rollout reports `successfully rolled out`, smoke suite is 100% green, `/healthz` returns the new version string, and staging logs show no new error signatures.","done":false}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/6","value":{"id":"s7","number":7,"title":"Canary the release to production, then ramp","stage":"Release","owner":"Deployer","minutes":8,"risk":"full-fleet outage","summary":"Send 5% of traffic to the new version, watch metrics, then ramp to 25%, 50%, 100%.","why":"A canary limits the blast radius of anything staging could not reproduce: real traffic shapes, real cache behaviour, real concurrency. Ramping in stages gives you a statistically meaningful error-rate and latency comparison between old and new pods before the majority of users are exposed, and it keeps a healthy old version running so rollback is a traffic shift rather than a rebuild.","ifSkipped":"A big-bang deploy exposes 100% of users to 100% of a bug simultaneously, and because every old pod is gone your only recovery path is a fresh rollout of the previous image — minutes of hard downtime instead of seconds of degraded traffic for 5% of users. Any defect that only appears under production load becomes a full outage rather than a caught canary.","commands":"kubectl config use-context prod-us-east-1\nkubectl config current-context           # MUST print prod-us-east-1\nkubectl argo rollouts set image api-gateway api=ghcr.io/acme/api-gateway:v2.14.0 -n prod\nkubectl argo rollouts get rollout api-gateway -n prod --watch\nkubectl argo rollouts promote api-gateway -n prod    # 5% -> 25%\nkubectl argo rollouts promote api-gateway -n prod    # 25% -> 50%\nkubectl argo rollouts promote api-gateway -n prod --full","verify":"At each stage: canary 5xx rate within 0.1% of baseline, p95 latency within 10% of baseline, no pod restarts. Only then promote.","done":false}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/7","value":{"id":"s8","number":8,"title":"Verify, watch for 30 minutes, then announce","stage":"Confirm","owner":"On-call","minutes":10,"risk":"silent regressions","summary":"Confirm the deployed version, watch the golden signals for a full bake period, and record the release.","why":"Most regressions do not appear in the first 60 seconds — they appear when caches expire, a cron fires, memory grows, or a connection pool saturates. A deliberate bake period with dashboards open is the difference between you finding the problem and a customer finding it. Announcing and logging the release closes the loop so the next on-call knows what changed when they get paged at 03:00.","ifSkipped":"You declare victory and walk away while a memory leak builds toward an OOM-kill an hour later, with nobody connecting the pager alert to your deploy. Skipping the changelog and announcement means the next responder debugs blind and may 'fix' the incident by rolling forward into the same bug.","commands":"curl -fsS https://api.acme.com/version | jq -r .gitSha    # matches your tag SHA\nkubectl get pods -n prod -l app=api-gateway -o wide       # all Running, 0 restarts\nkubectl logs -n prod -l app=api-gateway --since=10m | grep -i -

