{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/0","value":{"id":"s1","num":1,"title":"Freeze the branch and confirm what you are shipping","goal":"Pin the exact commit that will become the release.","phase":"Prepare","time":"3 min","risk":"High","owner":"Deploy lead","why":"A deploy is only reversible if you know precisely which commit is live. Freezing main and reading the diff between the last tag and HEAD tells you what behaviour is about to change, which migrations are included, and whether any feature flag needs flipping. It also stops a teammate from merging a half-finished PR into the release while you are mid-deploy.","skip":"You deploy an unknown set of changes. When errors spike you cannot tell which commit caused them, rollback targets are guesswork, and an unfinished PR that was merged 40 seconds before your build can reach real users. Post-incident reviews become impossible because there is no immutable reference point.","commands":"git checkout main
git pull --ff-only origin main
git fetch --tags
git log --oneline $(git describe --tags --abbrev=0)..HEAD
git diff --stat $(git describe --tags --abbrev=0)..HEAD","verify":"The log output lists only PRs you intend to release, and `git status` reports a clean tree on main.","done":false}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/1","value":{"id":"s2","num":2,"title":"Run the full test suite and linters","goal":"Prove the frozen commit is green before spending time on a build.","phase":"Verify","time":"6 min","risk":"High","owner":"Any engineer","why":"CI runs on the merge commit, but running locally catches environment-specific failures and flaky tests before you burn a release slot. Type checks and lint rules catch entire classes of runtime crashes — undefined properties, wrong argument counts, unused async — that unit tests alone miss. Green tests are also the evidence you attach to the release notes.","skip":"A broken build gets promoted. Because build artifacts are cached and immutable, you will discover the failure only after production traffic hits it, meaning a rollback plus a hotfix cycle instead of a two-minute local fix. Worst case, a failing migration test means data is already corrupted by the time you notice.","commands":"npm ci
npm run lint
npm run typecheck
npm test -- --run --coverage
npm audit --audit-level=high","verify":"All suites report pass, coverage has not dropped below your threshold, and audit shows no unresolved high/critical advisories.","done":false}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/2","value":{"id":"s3","num":3,"title":"Build the production artifact and tag the release","goal":"Produce one immutable, versioned image used in every environment.","phase":"Build","time":"5 min","risk":"Medium","owner":"CI / deploy lead","why":"Building once and promoting the same digest to staging and production removes the biggest source of 'it worked on staging' bugs. Production builds also apply minification, tree-shaking, dead-code elimination and NODE_ENV=production code paths that development builds never exercise. Tagging the image with the git SHA makes every running container traceable back to source.","skip":"Staging and production run different bytes, so staging validation proves nothing. You lose traceability: nobody can answer 'which commit is in this container?' Rollback becomes a rebuild from source, which can take 10+ minutes during an outage — and may not even reproduce if a dependency published a new version.","commands":"export VERSION=$(date +%Y.%m.%d)-$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)
npm run build
docker build -t registry.example.com/app:$VERSION -t registry.example.com/app:latest .
docker push registry.example.com/app:$VERSION
git tag -a v$VERSION -m "Release $VERSION"
git push origin v$VERSION","verify":"`docker images | grep $VERSION` shows the image, the registry lists the new tag, and the git tag exists on the remote.","done":false}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/3","value":{"id":"s4","num":4,"title":"Take a verified database backup","goal":"Guarantee you can restore state if a migration goes wrong.","phase":"Safety net","time":"4 min","risk":"Critical","owner":"Deploy lead","why":"Code rollbacks are cheap; data loss is permanent. A migration that drops a column, rewrites rows or adds a bad constraint cannot be undone by redeploying the old image. Taking the backup immediately before the migration minimises the recovery window, and restoring it into a scratch database is the only way to know the dump is actually readable.","skip":"An incorrect migration destroys customer data with no path back. You are left reconstructing rows from logs and support tickets, which usually means hours of downtime, contractual breach on your uptime SLA, and possible regulatory reporting obligations. Backups you never test frequently turn out to be empty or truncated.","commands":"export STAMP=$(date +%Y%m%dT%H%M%S)
pg_dump "$DATABASE_URL" --format=custom --no-owner --file=backup-$STAMP.dump
ls -lh backup-$STAMP.dump
pg_restore --list backup-$STAMP.dump | head -20
aws s3 cp backup-$STAMP.dump s3://example-backups/prod/","verify":"The dump is non-trivial in size, `pg_restore --list` prints the expected tables, and the object appears in S3.","done":false}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/4","value":{"id":"s5","num":5,"title":"Run database migrations (backward compatible only)","goal":"Move the schema forward without breaking the currently running code.","phase":"Migrate","time":"3 min","risk":"Critical","owner":"Deploy lead","why":"During a rolling deploy, old and new application versions run simultaneously, so the schema must satisfy both. That means additive changes only: add nullable columns, add indexes concurrently, backfill in batches, and defer destructive drops to a later release. Running the migration in a dry-run first shows you the generated SQL and any locking risk before it touches production.","skip":"Old pods start throwing 500s the instant the schema changes under them, or the migration takes an exclusive lock and every write request queues until it times out. Deploying app code before its migration is worse: the new code queries columns that do not exist yet, so the release fails closed for every user.","commands":"npm run migrate:status
npm run migrate:dry-run
npm run migrate:up -- --lock-timeout=5s --statement-timeout=30s
npm run migrate:status","verify":"Status shows every migration as applied, no long-running locks in `pg_stat_activity`, and error rates on the current version stay flat.","done":false}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/5","value":{"id":"s6","num":6,"title":"Deploy to staging and smoke test","goal":"Exercise the real artifact against real infrastructure before users see it.","phase":"Staging","time":"8 min","risk":"High","owner":"QA + deploy lead","why":"Staging is where configuration bugs surface: missing environment variables, wrong secret names, misconfigured CDN origins, broken third-party callbacks, incorrect health-check paths. Unit tests cannot catch any of these because they never boot the container in a cluster. A short scripted smoke test over login, the primary write path and the checkout/critical flow catches 90% of deploy-time regressions in under five minutes.","skip":"Production becomes your test environment. Config mistakes take the whole site down rather than a staging URL nobody depends on, and you debug under time pressure with customers watching. You also lose the chance to have QA sign off, so accountability for the release is unclear.","commands":"kubectl config use-context staging
kubectl set image deployment/app app=registry.example.com/app:$VERSION -n staging
kubectl rollout status deployment/app -n staging --timeout=180s
curl -fsS https://staging.example.com/healthz
npm run test:e2e -- --base-url=https://staging.example.com","verify":"Rollout completes, /healthz returns 200 with the new version string, and the e2e smoke suite passes.","done":false}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/6","value":{"id":"s7","num":7,"title":"Deploy to production gradually (canary, then full)","goal":"Limit blast radius by exposing the release to a small slice of traffic first.","phase":"Release","time":"10 min","risk":"Critical","owner":"Deploy lead","why":"Some failures only appear at production scale or with production data: hot partitions, N+1 queries against large tables, cache stampedes, rate limits on a paid API. Sending 5–10% of traffic to the new version for a few minutes surfaces those with a tenth of the impact, and a maxUnavailable=0 rolling strategy means healthy old pods keep serving while new ones pass readiness checks.","skip":"A latent bug hits 100% of users at once. Instead of a handful of retried requests you get a full outage, a spike in support volume, and possibly cascading failures in downstream services. Recovery also takes longer because every pod must be rolled back rather than just the canary.","commands":"kubectl config use-context production
kubectl apply -f k8s/canary.yaml --set image.tag=$VERSION
kubectl rollout status deployment/app-canary -n prod --timeout=120s
# watch for 5 minutes, then promote
kubectl set image deployment/app app=registry.example.com/app:$VERSION -n prod
kubectl rollout status deployment/app -n prod --timeout=300s
kubectl get pods -n prod -l app=app","verify":"Canary error rate and p95 latency match the baseline, then all pods report Running/Ready on the new image digest.","done":false}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/7","value":{"id":"s8","num":8,"title":"Watch monitoring for 15 minutes and warm caches","goal":"Confirm the release is healthy under real traffic, not just booted.","phase":"Observe","time":"15 min","risk":"High","owner":"Deploy lead + on-call","why":"'Pods are Ready' only means the health endpoint answered. The signals that matter are error rate, p95/p99 latency, saturation (CPU, memory, DB connections) and business metrics like signups or orders per minute. Most regressions declare themselves within ten minutes as caches cool, queues drain and cron jobs fire. Purging the CDN prevents users from getting a stale HTML shell that requests deleted JS bundles.","skip":"You declare victory and walk away while a memory leak, connection-pool exhaustion or 4xx spike builds silently. The first person to notice is a customer hours later, by which point the deploy is no longer the obvious suspect and mean time to recovery balloons. Stale CDN assets produce mysterious white screens for cached visitors.","commands":"cdn purge --zone example.com --everything
kubectl logs -n prod -l app=app --since=15m --tail=200 | grep -Ei 'error|fatal|panic'
kubectl top pods -n prod
curl -fsS https://example.com/healthz | jq .version
open "https://grafana.example.com/d/app-overview?from=now-30m"","verify":"Error rate under 0.5%, p95 latency within 10% of pre-deploy baseline, memory flat, and /healthz reports the new version.","done":false}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/8","value":{"id":"s9","num":9,"title":"Announce, document, and close the release","goal":"Leave a written trail so the next person can debug without you.","phase":"Wrap up","time":"5 min","risk":"Medium","owner":"Deploy lead","why":"Release notes turn an opaque event into searchable history: when something regresses next week, the first question is always 'what changed?'. Publishing the GitHub release, unfreezing main, and posting in the deploy channel also tells support and on-call what to expect and gives them the rollback command if you are offline. Recording the deploy timestamp lets monitoring tools correlate anomalies with the change.","skip":"Institutional memory evaporates. Support triages user reports without knowing a release just landed, on-call cannot roll back because the previous tag is undocumented, and two engineers may try to deploy at once because the freeze was never lifted. Compliance audits also require a change record for each production change.","commands":"gh release create v$VERSION --generate-notes --target main
git push origin main
# post to the deploy channel
curl -X POST -H 'Content-type: application/json' \
  --data "{\"text\":\"Deployed v$VERSION to production. Rollback: kubectl rollout undo deployment/app -n prod\"}" \
  "$SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL"","verify":"The GitHub release is public, the channel message is posted, and the deploy freeze label is removed from the repo.","done":false}}
