## Mode & scope decision

No DTO was provided, so this is a **non-DTO** build. I first tested it against the card budget: six input fields + a submit action cannot converge into a summary card (≤ 3 main sections, ≤ 1/3 screen height) without hiding fields you explicitly asked for. Convergence fails, so I am **explicitly escalating to Mode 3 — Non-DTO Page**. This is a form page, not a card.

## Layout rationale

- **Main sections (4):** ① accent header block (icon tile + title/subtitle) ② *Required* group — full name, phone, treatment ③ *Optional* group — email, preferred date, notes ④ full-width submit CTA + phone-callback reassurance footnote.
- **Visual focal point:** the teal icon tile + 40px title at the top, resolving into the solid teal CTA at the bottom — one entry point, one exit point.
- **Information rhythm:** required → divider → optional → action. Grouping tells the patient up front that only three answers are mandatory, so the form reads shorter than it is.
- **Key horizontal relationships:** every `Row` holds exactly 2 children (icon+titles, label+status pill, section title+count pill, footnote icon+text). Field labels sit *above* inputs vertically — no label/input side-by-side, which is the main narrow-screen overflow risk. Status pills carry `flex-shrink: 0` so "Required" / "Optional" never fragment.
- **Images:** none fabricated. I used built-in `Icon` (`calendarToday`, `call`) instead of inventing image URLs.
- **Improvement round applied:** the first draft was a flat stack of six identical labelled fields with a bare submit button. I reworked it into Required/Optional groups with status pills, added a helper line under *Anything else*, gave the notes field a longText variant, added the accent header tile for hierarchy, and added the callback reassurance line so the form ends on trust rather than on a button.

