## Layout Rationale

**Mode**: Non-DTO Component (Mode 2) — a compact summary card, not a page.

- **Main sections (3, within budget)**: ① header block (accent icon + title/subtitle) → ② the data table → ③ a single-line footnote with the measurement caveat.
- **Visual focal point**: the `Table`. It is the reason the card exists, so it gets the largest visual mass and sits directly below the header with no competing elements.
- **Information rhythm**: tight identity header → dense structured data → light, low-contrast footnote. Weight decreases top-to-bottom so the eye lands on the table.
- **Key horizontal relationships**: (a) header row = color-block icon (fixed, protected) + text column (compressible, wraps); 2 children only. (b) table locked to **3 columns** — rank / country / area. A 4th "share of land" column was deliberately dropped because 4 columns of numeric text risk clipping on a narrow screen.
- **Role of images**: none. There is no trustworthy real image URL for this query, so instead of fabricating one I use a `locationOn` icon inside a tinted color block as the visual anchor — this also avoids a full-width hero image, which is forbidden in card mode.
- **Component expressiveness**: `Table` (Tier 2) is the correct choice over stacked `Text` rows, since the data is genuinely tabular. A `Chart` would be over-engineering for 3 values whose exact figures matter more than their proportion.

**Improvement round applied** (first draft → final): the first draft was a bare `Card` + `Table` with a plain text title. Changes made for premium feel: added the tinted icon color block as a visual anchor, split the header into title + subtitle for hierarchy, introduced a `Divider` to separate authoritative data from the caveat, converged font sizes to the unified scale (36 / 32 / 28 / 24), and added outer whitespace so the drop-shadow does not touch the edge.

