## Mode & scope decision

**Mode 3 — Non-DTO Page** (explicit escalation, not a silent one).

You asked for: a step list **plus**, for every step, (a) why it matters, (b) the failure mode if skipped, and (c) exact runnable commands. That is 6 steps × 3 explanatory blocks + code blocks. A component/card is hard-capped at ≤ 3 main sections, ≤ 2 short body paragraphs, no dynamic list templates, and ≤ 1/3 screen height. Converging your request into a card would delete the detail you explicitly requested. Per **user requirements first**, I escalate to a page rather than mutilate the content.

## Layout rationale (before any JSON)

- **Main sections (5):** page header → "run in order" advisory → step runbook list (dynamic, template-driven) → rollback block → footnote.
- **Visual focal point:** the indigo numbered step badge column; the eye scans the number rail vertically, so order is legible before any reading happens.
- **Information rhythm per step:** dense heading (36px) → light label (24px, indigo) → readable prose (32px) → amber risk panel → neutral code panel. Each step repeats the same 3-beat rhythm so the page becomes scannable rather than a wall of text.
- **Key horizontal relationships:** only 2-child rows — `badge | title-block` (badge is the protected fixed 56px column, title absorbs all compression) and `icon | label` pairs. No row exceeds 3 children, no tag group ever shares a row with a CTA.
- **Role of visuals:** no decorative imagery (I will not fabricate image URLs). Visual weight is carried by tinted sectioning blocks (amber = danger, slate = terminal) and `Markdown` fenced code, which is the correct Tier-2 component for commands — plain `Text` would lose monospacing and copyability.

**Improvement round applied (1×, mandatory):** the first draft had the "why / if you skip / commands" text as three flat `Text` runs. That read as an undifferentiated paragraph pile. I upgraded it to semantic tinted panels (amber warning panel with `warning` icon for the skip-consequence, neutral bordered `Markdown` panel for commands) and moved commands out of `Text` into `Markdown` so code renders as code. Code panels use a **light** neutral background, not dark, because `Markdown` inherits dark default text and would be unreadable on a dark fill.

