{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/0","value":{"id":"1","title":"Confirm the correct branch and latest changes","command":"git checkout main && git pull origin main && git status","why":"This ensures the deployment is built from the approved, current production branch. Pulling first incorporates reviewed changes already merged by the team, while git status confirms that no uncommitted local edits will accidentally enter the release.","skipRisk":"Skipping this can deploy an outdated commit, an unintended feature branch, or local modifications that were never reviewed. It also makes it difficult to reproduce exactly what reached production."}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/1","value":{"id":"2","title":"Install locked dependencies","command":"npm ci","why":"npm ci installs the exact package versions recorded in package-lock.json. It creates a clean, reproducible dependency tree that matches CI and avoids unexpected updates from version ranges in package.json.","skipRisk":"Skipping this may leave stale or missing dependencies from a previous build. Using a non-locked install can produce a different artifact from CI, leading to production-only failures."}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/2","value":{"id":"3","title":"Run quality checks and tests","command":"npm run lint && npm test","why":"Linting catches static errors and consistency problems, while the test suite validates expected application behavior before users are affected. Running both immediately before deployment provides a final release gate.","skipRisk":"Skipping checks can publish syntax errors, broken imports, failed business logic, or regressions that automated tests would have detected in minutes."}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/3","value":{"id":"4","title":"Build the production artifact","command":"npm run build","why":"The production build compiles, optimizes, and validates the application using production settings. It is the artifact that should be deployed, rather than source files or a development server output.","skipRisk":"Skipping the build can result in missing assets, unoptimized code, unresolved environment configuration, or a deployment that fails only after reaching the hosting platform."}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/4","value":{"id":"5","title":"Deploy the approved build","command":"npm run deploy -- --environment=production","why":"This command publishes the prepared production artifact to the production environment. Passing the environment explicitly reduces the chance of targeting a staging or personal environment by mistake.","skipRisk":"Skipping the explicit environment can send the release to the wrong target or leave production running an old version. Deploying without a validated build risks publishing an incomplete artifact."}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/5","value":{"id":"6","title":"Verify the live release and monitor errors","command":"curl -I https://app.example.com && npm run logs -- --environment=production --tail=100","why":"A live HTTP check confirms that the application is reachable after deployment. Reviewing recent logs identifies startup failures, configuration errors, and elevated error rates before they affect more users.","skipRisk":"Skipping verification can leave a broken deployment unnoticed. DNS, routing, environment variables, migrations, and runtime dependencies can all fail despite a successful deploy command."}}
