{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/0","value":{"id":"1","number":"Step 1","title":"Update and inspect the codebase","command":"git checkout main && git pull origin main && git status","why":"Deploying from an up-to-date, clean main branch ensures the artifact you publish contains the approved code. It also prevents accidentally releasing uncommitted local changes or an outdated branch.","skip":"Skipping this step can deploy stale code, omit a teammate's fix, or include unintended work. These errors are especially difficult to trace once the release is live."}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/1","value":{"id":"2","number":"Step 2","title":"Install locked dependencies","command":"npm ci","why":"npm ci installs the exact dependency versions recorded in package-lock.json. This makes the local build reproducible and closely matches what a continuous deployment environment will install.","skip":"Using a loose install or skipping dependency installation can produce a build that differs from production. A missing or unexpectedly upgraded package may cause runtime failures."}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/2","value":{"id":"3","number":"Step 3","title":"Run tests and quality checks","command":"npm run lint && npm test","why":"Linting catches consistency and static-analysis issues, while tests verify expected behavior before users encounter the release. Running both creates a quality gate before the code is published.","skip":"If you skip validation, regressions, broken imports, and failing business logic can reach production. Rollbacks then consume time and may interrupt users."}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/3","value":{"id":"4","number":"Step 4","title":"Build the production artifact","command":"npm run build","why":"The production build compiles, optimizes, and bundles the application exactly as it should run in production. This is where framework-specific configuration and build-time environment issues are exposed.","skip":"A deployment can fail during the provider build, or worse, ship an unoptimized or incomplete artifact. Testing the build locally shortens the feedback loop."}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/4","value":{"id":"5","number":"Step 5","title":"Configure production environment variables","command":"vercel env pull .env.production.local && cat .env.production.local","why":"Production services need credentials, API endpoints, and feature configuration that should never be committed to source control. Pulling and inspecting the configured environment confirms the deployment has the values it needs.","skip":"Missing variables commonly cause startup failures, authentication errors, unavailable integrations, or connections to the wrong service. Never commit the resulting environment file."}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/5","value":{"id":"6","number":"Step 6","title":"Deploy to production","command":"vercel --prod","why":"This command creates a production deployment using the built application and configured project settings. The deployment provider assigns the release to the production domain after a successful build.","skip":"Without executing the deployment, all local preparation remains unavailable to users. Deploying without the production flag may only create a preview URL rather than updating the live site."}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/steps/6","value":{"id":"7","number":"Step 7","title":"Verify the live release and monitor","command":"curl -I https://your-domain.com && vercel logs --prod","why":"A live HTTP check confirms that the production domain responds successfully, while logs reveal startup errors and runtime exceptions that may not appear in build output. Verification closes the release loop.","skip":"A deployment can report success while the user-facing service has a routing, configuration, or runtime problem. Without monitoring, issues may remain undetected until customers report them."}}
