{"op":"add","path":"/state/deploymentSteps/0","value":{"id":"validate","number":"Step 1","title":"Validate the application locally","summary":"Confirm that the application builds, tests pass, and required environment variables are present before creating a deployable image.","commands":"# Install project dependencies
npm ci

# Run automated tests
npm test

# Build the production bundle
npm run build

# Check required deployment variables
printenv | grep -E '^(DATABASE_URL|API_KEY|NODE_ENV)='","why":"Local validation catches broken builds, missing dependencies, failing tests, and configuration mistakes while feedback is fast and inexpensive. The production build is especially important because it can expose errors that do not appear in development mode.","skip":"If you skip validation, you may publish an image that crashes on startup, serves an incomplete bundle, or fails only after it reaches your users. Rollbacks then take longer because the failure was not isolated before release.","outcome":"You have a passing test suite, a successful production build, and a documented list of environment variables that the deployed service needs."}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/deploymentSteps/1","value":{"id":"image","number":"Step 2","title":"Build and tag an immutable container image","summary":"Package the validated application into a Docker image and give the release a unique, traceable tag.","commands":"# Build a release image from the current commit
export IMAGE=registry.example.com/my-team/my-app
export TAG=$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)
docker build --tag ${IMAGE}:${TAG} .

# Optional: test the image locally
docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 --env-file .env.production ${IMAGE}:${TAG}

# Confirm the tag that will be deployed
echo ${IMAGE}:${TAG}","why":"A container image packages the exact application code, runtime, and dependencies that were tested. Tagging it with the commit SHA makes the release reproducible and lets your team identify precisely what is running.","skip":"Using an untagged or mutable latest image makes deployments difficult to audit and roll back. Building directly in a cluster can also create environment-dependent behavior and makes it harder to reproduce a release.","outcome":"You have a locally tested image referenced by a stable, unique tag such as registry.example.com/my-team/my-app:a1b2c3d."}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/deploymentSteps/2","value":{"id":"publish","number":"Step 3","title":"Publish the image to the container registry","summary":"Authenticate to the registry and push the immutable image so the Kubernetes cluster can retrieve it.","commands":"# Authenticate with your registry
docker login registry.example.com

# Push the exact release tag
docker push ${IMAGE}:${TAG}

# Inspect the remote image metadata (requires crane)
crane manifest ${IMAGE}:${TAG}","why":"Your cluster needs a durable, network-accessible image source. Publishing the same tagged artifact that you tested ensures the deployment uses the intended release rather than a rebuild from potentially changed source code.","skip":"If the image is not published or the cluster lacks permission to pull it, Kubernetes will create pods that remain in ImagePullBackOff. Deployments cannot become healthy until the registry image and credentials are available.","outcome":"The release image exists in the registry and can be pulled by the cluster's service account or image pull secret."}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/deploymentSteps/3","value":{"id":"release","number":"Step 4","title":"Apply the Kubernetes release and verify rollout","summary":"Update the deployment with the published image, wait for the rollout to finish, then inspect the service and logs.","commands":"# Select the target cluster and namespace
kubectl config use-context production-cluster
kubectl create namespace my-team --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -

# Create or update application configuration
kubectl -n my-team create secret generic my-app-secrets \
  --from-literal=DATABASE_URL="$DATABASE_URL" \
  --from-literal=API_KEY="$API_KEY" \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -

# Deploy the immutable release image
kubectl -n my-team create deployment my-app \
  --image=${IMAGE}:${TAG} \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -

# Expose the application internally
kubectl -n my-team expose deployment my-app --port=8080 --target-port=8080 --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -

# Wait for rollout and inspect health
kubectl -n my-team rollout status deployment/my-app --timeout=5m
kubectl -n my-team get pods,svc
kubectl -n my-team logs deployment/my-app --tail=100","why":"Applying declarative resources makes the desired production state explicit. Waiting for the rollout verifies that Kubernetes can schedule, pull, start, and keep the new version healthy. Inspecting logs provides immediate confirmation that configuration and startup behavior are correct.","skip":"Without rollout verification, a command can return successfully even though pods are failing, unavailable, or serving an old version. Skipping service checks can leave a healthy pod unreachable by the rest of your system.","outcome":"The deployment reports successfully rolled out, ready pods are running the selected image tag, the service has endpoints, and logs show a clean startup."}}
